Chirality is an eminent feature of nature and plays an indispensable role in biological functions and material sciences. Designed synthesis of homochiral metal organic frameworks (MOFs) or coordination polymers (CPs) has been a frontier research field in recent years due to their prime importance in the field of asymmetric catalysis, magnetism, non–linear optics and chiral recognition.1–4 The straight forward way to synthesize homochiral ${\mathrm{CPs/MOFs}}$ is the use of enantiopure building blocks which translate their inherent chirality to the resultant framework by “chirality conservation” process. This methodology suffers from the high costs of enantiopure organic linkers. However, there are a few excellent reports on naturally occurring chiral organic molecules as linker for the construction of homochiral MOFs.3,4 The prime indirect ways to synthesize chiral MOFs by achiral building blocks are ‘spontaneous resolution’ and ‘chirality induction’(CI).1 The homochiral crystallization of ${\mathrm{CPs/MOFs}}$ from achiral building blocks still remains a scantily explored area. Self assembly of chiral MOFs consisting of achiral building blocks by means of spontaneous resolution is an interesting phenomenon.5 However, the handedness of individual nuclei during crystallization remains uncontrollable in the case of spontaneous resolution and the products tend to be conglomerate except in a few cases where a rare course of symmetry breaking operates.6 These unresolved conglomerates remain of less practical use. The other methodology, i.e., the use of chiral induction agents to achieve bulk homochiral MOFs is a powerful strategy as it can accomplish absolute bulk chirality in the system. Camphoric acid and cinchona alkaloids are most celebrated chiral inducing agents.7 The use of cinchona alkaloids as chiral induction agent was first demonstrated by Zhang, et al. for the construction of homochiral microporous MOFs.7a Recently Duan et.al, have further explored the cinchona alkaloids as chiral induction agent to design catalytically active homochiral CPs from achiral precursors.7b Moreover, there are a couple of examples in which an enantiopure solvent has smartly been employed as chirality induction agent.8,9
In a remarkable report, Bu et al, exploited the chirality induction competence of camphoric acid to direct the absolute chirality of intrinsically chiral MOF composed of achiral building blocks.10 They have shown that the racemic form of camphoric acid induces the  formation of conglomerate MOF; however, enantiopure camphoric acid could induce absolute bulk chirality in the same MOFs. Interestingly, a different achiral MOF was obtained in the absence of chiral induction agents.
Scheme 1.  Cartoon depiction of the chiral induction (CI) agent driven conglomeration and bulk asymmetric crystallization processes (for example, reference 10) (a); spontaneous resolution driven conglomeration and the asymmetric coercing  of same process via CI agent leading to absolute asymmetric crystallization (present study) (b).
Though there have been a few excellent reports demonstrating the phenomenon of chiral induction to synthesize bulk homochiral MOFs, the method remains elusive due to the unpredictability of suitable chiral induction agent for a given set of precursors.11 Aforesaid elegant reports indicate that the chirality induction agents work specifically on individual systems and often remain seminal for the construction of desired network. Evidently, there is a realistic requirement to develop protocols to synthesize bulk homochiral MOFs.
In our pursuit of novel chiral CPs/MOFs comprising of achiral building blocks, we accentuate on the cooperative operation of spontaneous resolution and chiral induction. To be precise, we have employed a chiral induction agent to maneuver the fate of reaction from spontaneous resolution to the bulk homochiral crystallization of CPs as depicted in Scheme 1. As far as we know there have been no reports on utilizing the enantiopure agents in crystallization process to yield bulk absolute chirality in CPs/MOFs from the achiral precursors which would otherwise yield spontaneous resolution in the absence of those enantiopure agents.
Scheme 2.  Depiction of studied crystallization processes: Contribution of enantiopure camphoric acid induces the absolute asymmetric crystallization of 2D homochiral CPs and absence of CI agent leads to conglomerate products.
Herein we report two enantiomeric pairs (1P, 1M and 2P, 2M) of isostructural 2–dimensional metal organic frameworks $\left[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathbf{L})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathbf{1})$ and $\mathrm{\small{[Co(SO_{4})(L)(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{n}(}}$ (2) (where $\mathbf{L}=$ 1,4–bis(4–pyridyl)–2,3–diaza–1,3–butadiene) comprising of achiral building blocks by the spatial orientation of the ligand moiety coordinated to the metal. Either of the $\bf{1}\mathrm{{P/1M}}$ and $\mathbf{2}\mathbf{P}/\mathbf{2}\mathbf{M}$ pairs of chiral MOFs can be synthesized as a conglomerate by spontaneous resolution occurred upon diffusing the alcoholic solution of ligand (L) into the aqueous metal sulphate solution. We further show that the enantiopure camphoric acid (cam) can act as chirality inducing agent to achieve bulk absolute chiral CPs with desired helicity (1P, 1M, 2P and 2M). In the chirality induction experiments enantiopure camphoric acid can coerce the enantio– enrichment of bulk samples. Thus, MOFs 1P and 2P with absolute positive homohelicity have been obtained in the presence of (—)–cam whereas $(+)$ –cam directs the exclusive formation of absolute negative homohelical CPs 1M and 2M (supporting information S1).
All the four compounds crystallize in chiral space group C2 and possess a two dimensional network with intrinsic chirality. The Zn and Co CPs are isotypic; hence the structural description is restricted to the Zn CPs 1P and 1M. 1P and 1M contain two crystallographically independent $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(SO_{4})(L)(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{n}}}$ entities with identical coordination environment (Figure S2). Coordination around the $Z\mathrm{n}^{+2}$ possesses a distorted octahedral N2O4 geometry. The basal plane of each metal center is provided by two cis coordinated water molecules and oxygen atoms from two different sulfato groups oriented in cis disposition linking the adjacent screw related $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ions via $\upmu_{2}-\upkappa^{2}$ binding mode to form a neutral zigzag $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(H_{2}O)_{2}(\upmu{-}S O_{4})]_{n}}}$ chain motif running along the b– axis (Figure 1a). The adjoining $\mathbf{M}^{\mathbf{\hdots}}\mathbf{M}$ distance within zigzag chain for both the molecules present in the asymmetric unit is 6.96 Å $(Z\mathrm{n}1^{\ldots}Z\mathrm{n}1)$ and $6.03\mathring\mathrm{A}$ $(Z\ n2{\cdots}Z\ n2)$ respectively. $\mathbf{\mathrm{{Zn^{...}O}}}$ distance involving the water ranges from 2.087(2) to $2.170(2)\mathrm{\AA}$ , 2.109(2) to $2.123(2)\mathrm{\AA}$ and the sulfato bridged oxygens is 2.071(2) to 2.098(2) Å, 2.150(2) Å, 2.165(2) $\textrm{\AA}$ apropos $Z\mathrm{n}1$ and $Z\mathrm{n}2$ respectively. The $\mathrm{[Zn(H_{2}O)_{2}(\upmu{-}S O_{4})]}$ chains running parallel to the b–axis are further coupled by $\mathbf{L}$ with alternate metal centre in cross chain mode fabricating a right (P) and left (M) handed helical coil in 1P and 1M respectively (Figures 1b, 2 and S3). Adjacent $\mathrm{[Zn(H_{2}O)_{2}(\upmu{-}S O_{4})]_{n}}$ zigzag chains are connected by the binodal ligands L resulting into $\mathrm{[Zn(\mu{-}S O_{4})(L)(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{n}}$ two dimensional network (Figure 1c) in which the same $\mathbf{M}^{\hdots}\mathbf{M}$ cross–chain distance $(\mathrm{Zn1^{...}Z n i/Z n2^{...}Z n2}=15.59\mathrm{\AA})$ is retained for both the molecules present in 1P. Accordingly, the trans axial position occupied by the terminal nitrogen atoms from two different L molecules completes the octahedral coordination around $\mathbf{M}^{+2}$ in which $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{n}\cdots N}$ distance range from 2.140(3) to 2.120(3) and 2.125(3) to 2.151(3) for $Z\mathrm{nl}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}2$ respectively. Average dihedral angle between the two peripheral pyridine rings of the rigid ligand $\mathbf{L}$ being $8.69^{\circ}$ and $8.78^{\circ}$ , indicating only marginal twist in these ligands to make effective coordination with the metal in both the zinc complexes.
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
In order to confirm the absolute configurations, individual crystals of 1P, 1M; and 2P, 2M used for crystallographic studies were further characterized by solid–state circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy, which furnished complementary results to the crystallographic findings. The Zn and Co CPs show electronic absorption spectra in the region ca $295~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S5). Hence, CD spectra for six random crystals from the both Zn and Co CPs crystallized in the presence of enantiopure camphoric acids and ten crystals from the each batch crystallized in the absence of camphoric acid were recorded using Nujol mull (Figure S6). Single crystals of both CPs grown in the presence of $(-)\cdot$ –cam and $(+)$ –cam showed a positive and negative dichroic signal at ca $295~\mathrm{nm}$ respectively. Representative CD patterns for 1P and 1M possess virtually mirror symmetric relation as depicted in Figure 3. Hence, the absolute chirality of the CPs prepared in the presence of enantiopure camphoric acid established by single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ –ray studies are also complemeted by the CD data. Randomly picked individual crystals from the batch crystallized in the absence of enantiopure camphoric acid showed positive/negative dichroic signals varying from crystal to crystal, indicating the formation of conglomerate (Figure S6). As anticipated, mixture of a few crystals from the batch crystallized in the absence of CI agent (cam) did not show any dichroic signal (Figure S6). Thus,CD spectral data also supports the conglomerate and predominent bulk homochiral crystallization of CPs in the absence or presence of enantiopure camphoric acid respectively. However, it is a challenging task to prove the absolute homochirality of the bulk sample because determination of absolute configuration in each and every crystal by single crystal X–ray diffraction is not viable.9 The results presented here i.e., 36 $(6{\times}6)$ good quality single crystal data with refined Flack parameter values indicate the crystallization experiments carried out in the presence of enantiopure camphoric acid results in the predominant formation of desired enantiomorph.
Both $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Co CPs showed thermal stability upto ca $280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after liberating the lattice guests in the temperature range 60–150 and $50{-}160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ respectively (Figure S7). Luminescent properties of both CPs were also explored and observed that upon excitation at $295~\mathrm{nm}$ ; both CPs emit at $365~\mathrm{nm}$ which could be assigned to the intra–ligand energy transfer. The overall quantum yields $(\Phi_{\mathrm{overall}})$ for $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Co CPs were 4.4 and 5.7 respectively which indicate the excellent fluorescent ability of these materials (Figure S8).
In summary, we demonstrated that the phenomenon of chirality induction can be applied to the CP/MOF systems comprising of achiral building blocks which otherwise display spontaneous resolution. Absolute chiral crystallization for the isostructural $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Co coordination polymeric enantiomorphs $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathbf{L})$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\]_{\mathrm{n}}$ and $\mathrm{\small{~\left[Co(SO_{4})(L)(H_{2}O)_{2}\right]_n}}$ has been achieved in the presence of chirality induction agent (enantiopure camphoric acids). In the absence of camphoric acid conglomerate formation was observed. The bulk homochiral crystallization and conglomerate formation studies for the system are unambiguously established by crystallography and CD spectroscopy. Chirality in 1P /1M and 2P /2M is originated by the self assembly of homo-helical motifs in the intrinsically chiral pseudo–Kagomé type 2D network. Synthesized $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Co CPs show strong photoluminescence originating from the ligand to ligand energy transfer. Present study highlights the feasibility of chirality induction process on the systems exhibiting spontaneous resolution and hence opens up new avenues for the selective crystallization of desired enantiomorphs in MOFs/CPs comprising of achiral ligands. Acquiring insight from the present results, we are currently exploring the feasibility of chiral induction techniques in ternary MOF/CP conglomerates involving achiral dicarboxylate and flexible N–donor ligands.
